  "resolved_items": [
    {
      "item": "direct judge requested adjudication",
      "resolution": "The moving-jump objection is mathematically valid under ordinary spatial measure. The target's predicate is a pointwise realization with reduced applicability. Its definitional theorem does not resolve that restriction.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed pages 4–5 retain integral conservation for discontinuous solutions. D002 requires HasDerivAt for every endpoint pair and time. For q(x,t)=1 when x>t and 0 otherwise, with identity flux and endpoints 0,1, ordinary spatial mass is 1 for t≤0, 1−t for 0<t<1, and 0 for t≥1."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge requested adjudication",
      "resolution": "The measure objection remains an evidence gap, rather than an established incorrect measure. The supplied direct dossier does not supply the missing instance expansion.",
      "primary_evidence": "D060 has type MeasureTheory.MeasureSpace Real and displayed body inferInstance in both complete dossiers. D022 merely projects its measure; no supplied declaration establishes interval-length normalization."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge classification is undetermined",
      "resolution": "Retain undetermined and accepted=false. The formula agrees structurally, but full source applicability and the disputed measure identification are not established. No faithful-stronger classification is justified.",
      "primary_evidence": "Equation (1.10), printed pages 4–5, and D001–D002, D015, D060 distinguish the printed balance, its intended discontinuous applicability, and the target's particular analytic realization."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge classification is undetermined",
      "resolution": "Retain undetermined on primary evidence, independently of agreement between judges. The blind translation accurately exposes the definitional equivalence and everywhere derivative requirement.",
      "primary_evidence": "The blind target and direct target have the same binders and analytic conditions after replacing LocalDef001 and LocalDef002 with D001 and D002."
    },
    {
      "item": "blind translator left dependency_coverage item D060 unclear",
      "resolution": "D060 fixes a particular measure-space instance, but its construction and normalization cannot be independently recovered from inferInstance alone. Preserve this uncertainty; neither its name nor its owner module proves the needed normalization.",
      "primary_evidence": "D060 supplies only inferInstance. D018 and D078 consume the measure selected through D022. The reuse ledger contains no validated interpretation resolving D060."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left dependency_coverage item D001 unclear",
      "resolution": "Declaration meaning is resolved: D001 is exactly D002 specialized to Fin m. It adds no conservation premise, regularity qualification, or exceptional-time convention. Its source correspondence inherits D002's limitations.",
      "primary_evidence": "D001 body: fun {m} q flux => NumStability.IsIntegralConservationLawSolution q flux."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left dependency_coverage item D002 unclear",
      "resolution": "Declaration meaning is resolved: interval integrability and the prescribed ordinary mass derivative hold for every real endpoint pair and time. This is not an almost-everywhere or time-integrated solution predicate.",
      "primary_evidence": "D002 explicitly binds ∀ (a b t : Real) and conjoins IntervalIntegrable with HasDerivAt using literal flux(q(a,t)) and flux(q(b,t))."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left dependency_coverage item D015 unclear",
      "resolution": "The reused derivative interpretation is confirmed from the supplied body. Its task-specific effect is an ordinary two-sided derivative at every t. Structural ContinuousSMul instances do not impose smoothness on q and do not weaken this derivative requirement.",
      "primary_evidence": "D015 uses HasDerivAtFilter with nhds x and pure x. D003, D019–D021, D075, D077, D080–D081 concern continuity of scalar multiplication on the ambient real vector space."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left dependency_coverage item D016 unclear",
      "resolution": "Iff has its ordinary logical meaning. Unfolding D001–D002 gives P↔P. This can serve as a definitional presentation of a formulation, but it establishes neither P for arbitrary inputs nor agreement between P and an independently specified source solution class.",
      "primary_evidence": "D016 is logical equivalence. The full target's right side is the body of D002 instantiated at Fin m, and D001 abbreviates that instantiation."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left semantic_checklist item C03 unclear",
      "resolution": "Endpoint scope and fixed-limit dependence pass. Universal ordinary time differentiability is explicit in Lean; its correspondence to the source's discontinuous interpretation remains unresolved.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 4 states the relation for any two points. D002 binds a,b,t universally, while only τ varies in the function supplied to HasDerivAt. Printed page 5 does not specify exceptional times."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left semantic_checklist item C04 unclear",
      "resolution": "No global classical smoothness of q or flux is imposed. Spatial integrability is an explicit meaningfulness condition. Nevertheless, ordinary differentiability of every interval mass at every time excludes moving jumps at endpoint crossings under ordinary spatial measure.",
      "primary_evidence": "D002 contains only IntervalIntegrable and HasDerivAt conditions. Section 1.1.2 attaches sufficient smoothness to deriving the PDE from the integral law; the transported-step mass has corners at t=0 and t=1."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left semantic_checklist item C05 unclear",
      "resolution": "The entire displayed vector flux balance is present inside P. Coverage of the source's broader discontinuous formulation is not established. The outer equivalence must not be counted as an independent conservation assertion.",
      "primary_evidence": "Equation (1.10) and D002 have the same integrand and endpoint-flux order. D001–D002 make the audited theorem a definitional equivalence."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left semantic_checklist item C07 unclear",
      "resolution": "The inner equality and subtraction direction pass. The outer biconditional is a correct unfolding characterization, with no additional substantive strength. Its logical form alone is not a reason to reject a source formulation.",
      "primary_evidence": "Equation (1.10) has first-endpoint flux minus second-endpoint flux. D014, D045, D059, D076 implement that componentwise subtraction; D016 relates definitionally identical conditions."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left semantic_checklist item C10 unclear",
      "resolution": "Finite vector dimensions and nonlinear-compatible fluxes are preserved. The zero-dimensional extension is harmless. Restricting the solution predicate through everywhere mass differentiability reduces applicability and cannot justify faithful-stronger.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed pages 1 and 4 describe m-component states and nonlinear fluxes. The target quantifies over all m : Nat and arbitrary vector flux functions, while D002 requires every-time HasDerivAt."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left semantic_checklist item C12 unclear",
      "resolution": "Separate predicate nonvacuity from theorem triviality. The zero state satisfies P with any flux because the endpoint states coincide. Under ordinary spatial measure, the nonconstant one-component state q(x,t)=x−t with identity flux also satisfies P. The theorem remains automatic even when P is false.",
      "primary_evidence": "D002 gives zero integrals and zero endpoint differences for q=0. For q=x−t under dx, both the mass derivative and endpoint-flux difference equal a−b. D001–D002 reduce the theorem to P↔P."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left semantic_checklist item N06 unclear",
      "resolution": "No solution existence, uniqueness, entropy, or well-posedness claim is asserted or required by the selected display. The substantive issue is the analytic solution class, especially ordinary derivative existence at endpoint-crossing times.",
      "primary_evidence": "The target has no existential solution binder. D002 requires derivative existence through HasDerivAt; printed pages 4–5 leave the discontinuous interpretation unspecified."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C04 unclear",
      "resolution": "The translation correctly distinguishes regularity of interval mass from regularity of q. Its every-time derivative requirement is a real restriction, not merely notation for the source's unspecified discontinuous interpretation.",
      "primary_evidence": "The blind translation specifies an ordinary two-sided derivative at every real time. D015 confirms this, and the transported-step mass supplies explicit failure at crossing times."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C05 unclear",
      "resolution": "A definitional presentation is permissible for a source that introduces a formulation. However, acceptance requires the defined condition to represent that formulation adequately; the supplied equivalence provides no such independent assurance.",
      "primary_evidence": "Section 1.1.1 introduces physical laws in integral form rather than a theorem derived from an explicit PDE hypothesis. The blind translation and D001–D002 identify the theorem as an unfolding equivalence."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C06 unclear",
      "resolution": "Derivative, orientation, vector operations, and integrability meanings are resolved at the supplied frontier. Measure identification remains unresolved. The direct judge's conditional standard-volume interpretation does not supply the missing declaration evidence.",
      "primary_evidence": "D015, D018, D043–D049, D059, D078 expose the relevant operations. D060 remains inferInstance, and D022 only selects its stored measure."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C10 unclear",
      "resolution": "Global real space and time are supported by the introductory q : R×R→R^m convention. No scalar-only or linear-flux specialization occurs. Exact admissible-state domains are unspecified, and everywhere mass differentiability remains the demonstrated applicability restriction.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 1 specifies q's global real arguments. Printed pages 3–4 introduce f(q) without an admissible-state subset. The target uses a total vector flux and D002's universal derivative condition."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C11 unclear",
      "resolution": "Reversed and coincident endpoints are handled correctly. Under ordinary spatial measure, failure at moving-jump endpoint crossings is definite: no choice of the value on the jump restores the nonexistent ordinary mass derivative. The source's precise replacement interpretation remains unspecified.",
      "primary_evidence": "D078 reverses sign when endpoints are exchanged and is zero when they coincide. For the unit-speed step on [0,1], the left and right mass slopes differ at t=0 and t=1. Printed page 5 retains integral conservation for discontinuous solutions."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C12 unclear",
      "resolution": "There is no impossible premise or reliance solely on m=0. P has positive-dimensional models. P↔P is logically automatic for both conserving and nonconserving inputs, so theorem validity must be separated from fidelity of the predicate.",
      "primary_evidence": "For m=1, q=0 satisfies D002 for any flux. Under ordinary spatial measure, q(x,t)=t with zero flux fails P on [0,1], while the equivalence still holds by D001–D002."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item N06 unclear",
      "resolution": "No missing existence or uniqueness conclusion is identified. The unresolved regularity correspondence concerns derivative existence of interval amounts at all times, not an additional smooth-PDE hypothesis.",
      "primary_evidence": "D002 contains no pointwise PDE condition or smoothness binder. Section 1.1.2 separates classical PDE derivation from integral conservation at discontinuities."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left implication lean_implies_source unclear",
      "resolution": "Retain unclear for source faithfulness. The exact unfolding theorem supplies no independent evidence that source-admissible states belong to P. Conditional on P and identification of the measure with dx, it gives the printed ordinary balance, but this does not establish full discontinuous applicability.",
      "primary_evidence": "D001–D002 reduce the theorem to P↔P. D060 leaves measure normalization unexpanded. Printed pages 4–5 include discontinuous solutions, whereas the ordinary transported-step mass fails D002 at crossing times."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left implication translation_implies_source unclear",
      "resolution": "Retain unclear for the same primary-evidence reasons. The blind translation accurately reports the exact theorem, but its definitional truth does not imply the substantive source interpretation or resolve the missing measure evidence.",
      "primary_evidence": "The translated conclusion is LocalDef001(q,flux) iff its identical expansion. Its D060 ambiguity and every-time derivative restriction are confirmed by the direct declarations."
    }
  ],
  "implications": {
    "lean_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "unclear",
      "reasoning": "The source introduces a conservation formulation, for which a definitional presentation is not intrinsically inappropriate. However, the exact theorem only unfolds P and establishes no independent correspondence between P and source-admissible conserved states. Assuming P and ordinary spatial measure yields the printed pointwise balance with correct components and signs. Full correspondence remains unsupported because the measure normalization is unexpanded and the source expressly includes discontinuous solutions while leaving their analytic interpretation unspecified. Under dx, an elementary transported step definitively fails P at endpoint-crossing times."
    },
    "source_implies_lean": {
      "verdict": "yes",
      "reasoning": "For the exact audited theorem, D001 and D002 unfold its type to ∀ m q flux, P(q,flux) ↔ P(q,flux). That equivalence holds independently of the source. This verdict concerns the theorem, not membership in P: it does not assert that source-admissible discontinuous states satisfy the everywhere ordinary derivative condition."
    }
  },
  "classification": "undetermined",
  "accepted": false,
  "remaining_uncertainties": [
    "D060's supplied inferInstance body does not expose the selected real measure's construction or normalization. Identification with ordinary spatial dx remains unverified within the allowed evidence.",
    "The selected source pages do not specify the derivative, exceptional-time, or trace interpretation that makes integral conservation applicable to discontinuous solutions.",
    "The supplied evidence does not establish that the pointwise predicate P adequately represents the full source formulation. Its correct definitional equivalence cannot settle that correspondence."
  ],
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "restricted-discontinuous-solution-applicability",
      "evidence": "Printed pages 4–5 retain integral conservation for discontinuous solutions. D002 and D015 require an ordinary derivative for every endpoint pair and time. Under dx, q(x,t)=1 for x>t and 0 otherwise, with identity flux, has mass on [0,1] equal to 1, 1−t, and 0 in the successive time regions separated by 0 and 1. The mass has corners at both crossing times.",
      "impact": "The predicate excludes a conservative moving discontinuity under the usual weak or time-integrated interpretation. This restriction is reduced applicability, not genuine stronger-theorem content. The source's unspecified analytic convention prevents a complete equivalence adjudication."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "unresolved-measure-evidence",
      "evidence": "D060 displays inferInstance only; D022 projects the stored measure; D018 and D078 use it throughout. No supplied reuse record or declaration resolves its normalization.",
      "impact": "The audit cannot independently confirm that the integrated quantity is the source's spatial amount. This is an evidence limitation, not proof of an incorrect installed measure."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "definitional-theorem-role",
      "evidence": "D001 abbreviates D002, whose body is exactly the target's right-hand condition. The audited proposition therefore unfolds to P↔P.",
      "impact": "The theorem is a valid unfolding characterization and can present a definition. Its logical truth neither establishes conservation for arbitrary inputs nor validates the source faithfulness of P."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "preserved-structure-and-nonvacuity",
      "evidence": "The supplied types and bodies preserve finite real vector states, coupled nonlinear-compatible fluxes, fixed arbitrary endpoints, spatial integration, and first-minus-second componentwise flux. The zero state gives a positive-dimensional model for any flux; under dx, q(x,t)=x−t with identity flux gives a nonconstant model.",
      "impact": "No sign error, averaging substitution, source-term insertion, scalar-only restriction, impossible premise, or global smoothness assumption on q was found."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The attached primary-source renderings and inline declarations support exact agreement of the displayed conservation structure and establish the target's precise logical and analytic meaning. Rechecking the disputed dependencies confirms the ordinary derivative restriction and definitional equivalence, while leaving D060's measure normalization unverified. The moving-step calculation resolves the applicability concern concretely under ordinary spatial measure, but the selected source does not specify its rigorous discontinuous interpretation. A definitional presentation is not automatically unfaithful, and additional derivative requirements are not genuine theorem strength. Accordingly, the literal reverse implication is yes, the substantive forward correspondence remains unclear, and the consistent classification is undetermined with acceptance withheld. No tools were used, and no supplied hashes were independently recomputed."
}
